{"id":"oxy-earnings-q3-2026","title":"OXY earnings — Occidental, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-11-09","html":"<p>Management sees steady output and a fast pay-down of debt on a plan built for $50 oil, and the thing that could spoil it is a sharp fall in oil before the debt and preferred stock are dealt with.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-11-09. Next report: not found. Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> Steady. Production of 1,427 thousand barrels of oil equivalent a day beat the guidance midpoint; the realised oil price fell 11% from Q2 to $85.91.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> Held on spending (Q4 capital about $1.3B, inside the original year range). Underlying 2026 production nudged up. New: a $6B principal debt target, expected during 2027.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> Lower oil while hedges cap the upside. The collars cost $187M in Q3.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> A slight lean up, on your tested edge only. It holds while price closes above the 200-day near $56.25; a close above the 50-day at $59.82 would confirm it.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>GAAP EPS $1.61, adjusted $1.91. Estimates were $1.41 (Webull) or $1.47 (24/7 Wall St). A beat on both bases.</li><li>Revenue $6.77B against $6.89B: a slight miss.</li><li>Domestic gas fetched only $1.61 per Mcf.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> Production of 1.43–1.47 million barrels of oil equivalent a day, including Gulf of America curtailments. Capital about $1.3B.</li><li><strong>2027, preliminary:</strong> Capital about $5.9B. Production roughly flat, oil flat to slightly up.</li><li><strong>Planning price:</strong> $50 WTI. That price anchors the dividend and the leverage target of about 1x debt to EBITDA.</li><li><strong>Debt:</strong> Principal debt is $10.0B, down from $20.4B at year-end. The new target is $6B during 2027; the CFO put interest cost at about $400M a year at that level.</li><li><strong>Gas marketing:</strong> Lower income expected as new Permian pipelines open in Q4 and narrow the price gap to the Gulf Coast.</li><li><strong>Low-carbon:</strong> The STRATOS direct air capture plant is under repair, with full start-up targeted for the end of 2026. Several CO2 projects wait on investment decisions in early 2027.</li><li><strong>In his words:</strong> &quot;we're able to deliver more with less.&quot; (Richard Jackson, President and CEO)</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Price volatility.</strong> The 10-Q says prices should stay volatile. The whole plan is set at $50 WTI against a realised $85.91.</li><li><strong>Gulf of America.</strong> Output fell to 110 from 144 thousand barrels of oil equivalent a day on a third-party pipeline constraint and an October hurricane. The constraint runs into Q4, and 2027 output there could dip on the timing of new water-injection wells.</li><li><strong>Hedge cost.</strong> Collars on 100 thousand barrels a day ($55 floor, $75.89 average ceiling) cost $187M in Q3.</li><li><strong>Permian gas spreads.</strong> Narrowing as new pipelines open. Domestic gas realised $1.61 per Mcf.</li><li><strong>International volumes.</strong> Production-sharing terms cut barrels in Oman and Algeria when prices are high; the 10-Q also ties lower international sales to the Middle East conflict.</li><li><strong>The preferred stock.</strong> Redemption is targeted for August 2029, and an analyst on the call noted that redeeming early carries a penalty.</li></ol>\n<p>The 10-Q states no material changes to risk factors.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-lower-oil-with-capped-upside-both-ways\">The big worry: lower oil with capped upside, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Oil stays high. WTI was $86.12 on the morning of Nov 11 (Euronews), far above the $50 plan and above the $75.89 collar ceiling.</li><li>The Strait of Hormuz stays restricted and OPEC+ holds. The seven core OPEC+ members kept December targets unchanged on Nov 1 (Enerdata).</li><li>Debt keeps falling. Each billion repaid lowers the oil price the company needs.</li><li>The collars roll off or reset higher. Their term: not found.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>A reopening. The EIA's October outlook has Brent sliding from $105 in Q4 2026 to $74 by Q4 2027.</li><li>A fall that stops above the floor. Between $55 and about $76 the collared barrels earn the market price; above $76 they give up the gain, which is where oil is now. Occidental is paying for insurance it has not needed.</li><li>More Gulf of America downtime, on top of the Q4 constraint already guided.</li><li>A hiking Fed. Futures put about 90% odds on a December rise, which leans on demand and on the cost of whatever debt is left to refinance.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Better than a year ago. Debt was halved in nine months with the chemical-business sale and free cash flow.</li><li>More cautious than its peers. Exxon and Chevron are buying back stock; Occidental is paying down debt, raising the dividend in measured steps ($0.28 a share after two 8% raises) and keeping buybacks opportunistic only.</li><li>Less upside than its peers above $76 a barrel on the hedged barrels, and more comfort below $55. Its corporate breakeven: not found.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Capital spending:</strong> Q4 about $1.3B; 2027 about $5.9B, with production flat.</li><li><strong>Debt:</strong> Principal $10.0B, from $20.4B at year-end. Target $6B during 2027.</li><li><strong>Dividend:</strong> $0.28 a share; future raises described as measured.</li><li><strong>Buybacks and preferred:</strong> Buybacks opportunistic only. Preferred redemption targeted for August 2029; the company will build cash ahead of that date.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Richard Jackson, President and CEO since June 1, 2026. <strong>He is five months into the job.</strong> Not a founder; he joined in 2003 and was chief operating officer. This was his second earnings call as CEO.</li><li><strong>Predecessor:</strong> Vicki Hollub retired after ten years as CEO (since 2016) and stays on the board. A former CEO on the board can steady a handover or crowd the new one; watch which.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Sunil Mathew, Senior Vice President and CFO. His start date: not found.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> The succession just happened and was announced a month ahead (May 1, 2026). No replacement for the chief operating officer role was named in that release.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> Chair and CEO are split; Jack Moore is board chairman. No dual-class shares found. Berkshire Hathaway is widely reported as the largest holder. Hart Energy reported its stake at nearly 29% (about 255.3M shares) with regulatory clearance to go as high as 50% and no plan to take control, but that article carried no year, so the current stake is not confirmed here. Who holds the preferred stock: not confirmed in a page opened this run.</li><li><strong>Recent changes or investigations:</strong> The CEO change above. No investigation found in the last 12 months.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> Less on the CEO than on one shareholder. A holder of that size can shape what the company does with its cash, and the preferred stock ranks ahead of you until 2029.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"OXY\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"OXY · daily · my lines (Jul–Oct retracement) 